WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.813
Parallel sessions and worktrees.

2
00:00:02.093 --> 00:00:07.640
Once work is decomposed, some steps are independent, and you can run them at the same time.

3
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.184
Each needs its own checkout, or the sessions’ edits collide.

4
00:00:11.464 --> 00:00:19.955
Git worktrees give you exactly that: additional working directories attached to one repository, each on its own branch, sharing history.

5
00:00:20.235 --> 00:00:35.509
In Claude Code, claude -w feature-auth starts a session in a new worktree; subagents can set isolation: worktree; and /batch splits a large mechanical change into units, each in its own worktree, after showing you the plan.

6
00:00:35.789 --> 00:00:39.245
The best-practices page notes a few practical details.

7
00:00:39.525 --> 00:00:42.768
A worktree is a fresh checkout, so gitignored files like.

8
00:00:43.048 --> 00:00:47.891
env aren’t there and dependencies aren’t installed; list small files to copy in a.

9
00:00:48.171 --> 00:00:51.435
worktreeinclude, and keep the install command in CLAUDE.

10
00:00:51.715 --> 00:00:52.525
md.

11
00:00:52.805 --> 00:01:00.656
Clean-up is mostly automatic for interactive sessions, but claude -p --worktree runs are yours to remove with git worktree remove.

12
00:01:00.936 --> 00:01:04.413
Then the constraint that actually binds: your review capacity.

13
00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:08.725
Three parallel sessions produce three diffs that all need a careful read.

14
00:01:09.005 --> 00:01:21.763
Parallelism pays off when the streams are truly independent (different modules, no shared interfaces) and each has strong automated checks, so your review is a confirmation rather than an investigation.

15
00:01:22.043 --> 00:01:31.856
When two streams touch the same code, sequence them or extract the shared change first; otherwise you inherit a merge that nobody checked against both specs.
